1
00:00:00,560 --> 00:00:04,080
Under the Mackinac Bridge, there's a shipwreck that took 10 men.

2
00:00:04,150 --> 00:00:07,700
Two oil pipelines carrying millions of gallons a day.

3
00:00:07,770 --> 00:00:10,410
A bridge worker who never came home.

4
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:14,020
And a waterfall that no one saw for thousands of years.

5
00:00:14,090 --> 00:00:18,680
All of it hidden in water up to 295 feet deep.

6
00:00:18,750 --> 00:00:21,880
Millions of people have driven across this bridge.

7
00:00:21,950 --> 00:00:24,620
Almost none of them know what's underneath it.

8
00:00:24,690 --> 00:00:26,710
So let's drain it.

9
00:00:26,780 --> 00:00:28,040
We can't really, of course.

10
00:00:28,110 --> 00:00:33,080
But lake-bed maps, sonar and divers have shown us what's down there.

11
00:00:33,150 --> 00:00:38,030
So we're going to lower the water, a few feet at a time, and watch what appears.

12
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:41,735
And at the very bottom is something that, if it ever broke,

13
00:00:41,755 --> 00:00:45,080
could spread oil along hundreds of miles of shoreline.

14
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:49,720
This is Great Lakes Files.

15
00:00:49,790 --> 00:00:53,630
We go looking for the places hidden in and under these lakes.

16
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:57,322
Most of the people watching these videos haven't subscribed yet,

17
00:00:57,342 --> 00:01:00,680
so if you like this kind of story, tap subscribe right now.

18
00:01:01,390 --> 00:01:06,769
It's free, it takes one second, and it's what keeps this channel going.

19
00:01:06,839 --> 00:01:08,899
Let's start the drain.

20
00:01:08,969 --> 00:01:10,159
10 feet.

21
00:01:10,229 --> 00:01:15,669
Off Mackinac Island, a jumble of ancient broken rock starts to break the surface.

22
00:01:15,739 --> 00:01:18,719
Divers call it the Rock Maze.

23
00:01:18,789 --> 00:01:20,009
20 feet.

24
00:01:20,079 --> 00:01:22,989
The shoreline creeps outward on both sides.

25
00:01:23,059 --> 00:01:24,669
The islands grow.

26
00:01:24,739 --> 00:01:29,429
The 5-mile gap between Michigan's two peninsulas starts to narrow.

27
00:01:29,499 --> 00:01:30,709
30 feet.

28
00:01:30,779 --> 00:01:34,639
Between the bridge towers, the water is still deep and dark.

29
00:01:34,709 --> 00:01:41,235
But a few miles to the east, just under the surface, something long is waiting.

30
00:01:41,305 --> 00:01:44,770
At 35 feet, a few miles east of the bridge,

31
00:01:44,790 --> 00:01:48,255
something long and dark breaks the surface.

32
00:01:48,325 --> 00:01:50,705
The top of a ship's hull.

33
00:01:50,775 --> 00:01:52,975
Her name is the Cedarville.

34
00:01:53,045 --> 00:01:55,455
588 feet long.

35
00:01:55,525 --> 00:01:57,213
On the morning of May 7, 1965,

36
00:01:57,233 --> 00:02:01,310
she was carrying more than 14,000 tons of limestone through the Straits,

37
00:02:01,330 --> 00:02:02,505
with 35 men on board.

38
00:02:06,325 --> 00:02:11,008
The fog that morning was so thick that two ships had already run aground near the Soo,

39
00:02:11,028 --> 00:02:13,305
and another had crashed into a lighthouse.

40
00:02:15,035 --> 00:02:17,835
The Cedarville kept going at full speed.

41
00:02:17,905 --> 00:02:20,995
On the radar, her officers could see ships ahead.

42
00:02:21,065 --> 00:02:25,965
A Norwegian freighter, the Topdalsfjord, was coming the other way.

43
00:02:26,035 --> 00:02:29,475
Each captain thought the other knew what he was going to do.

44
00:02:29,545 --> 00:02:31,355
Neither did.

45
00:02:31,425 --> 00:02:34,115
The lookout shouted, "There she is!"

46
00:02:34,185 --> 00:02:37,158
The Norwegian ship's bow, built strong for breaking ice,

47
00:02:37,178 --> 00:02:41,165
came out of the fog and tore a hole in the Cedarville, below the waterline.

48
00:02:43,325 --> 00:02:46,105
The crew tried to cover the hole with a tarp.

49
00:02:46,175 --> 00:02:47,455
It was too big.

50
00:02:47,525 --> 00:02:51,012
The captain turned toward a beach about 4 miles away,

51
00:02:51,032 --> 00:02:53,725
to run her aground before she could sink.

52
00:02:53,795 --> 00:02:55,115
She didn't make it.

53
00:02:55,185 --> 00:02:58,863
At 10:25 that morning, still 2 miles short of the beach,

54
00:02:58,883 --> 00:03:02,165
the Cedarville rolled onto her side and went down.

55
00:03:03,745 --> 00:03:07,235
10 of the 35 men died.

56
00:03:07,305 --> 00:03:13,665
With the water gone, you'd see her lying on the bottom in two pieces, 110 feet down.

57
00:03:13,735 --> 00:03:18,767
Since then, 3 divers have lost their lives exploring her.

58
00:03:18,837 --> 00:03:20,591
At 65 feet, just west of the bridge,

59
00:03:20,611 --> 00:03:24,683
two dark lines come up out of the lake bed and run across the bottom, side by side,

60
00:03:24,703 --> 00:03:25,817
toward the other shore.

61
00:03:30,047 --> 00:03:32,227
They're oil pipelines.

62
00:03:32,297 --> 00:03:34,697
Together they're called Line 5.

63
00:03:34,767 --> 00:03:41,457
They were laid across the Straits in 1953, four years before the bridge was finished.

64
00:03:41,527 --> 00:03:44,397
Each pipe is 20 inches wide.

65
00:03:44,467 --> 00:03:50,017
They run 4.5 miles across the lake bed, from one peninsula to the other.

66
00:03:50,087 --> 00:03:50,740
Every day,

67
00:03:50,760 --> 00:03:57,067
they carry nearly 23 million gallons of crude oil and natural gas liquids from western Canada.

68
00:03:57,617 --> 00:04:00,567
Close to shore, the pipes are buried.

69
00:04:00,637 --> 00:04:06,078
But out here, past 65 feet, they simply lie on the bottom, in the open,

70
00:04:06,098 --> 00:04:07,617
held up on supports.

71
00:04:08,937 --> 00:04:10,097
Remember them.

72
00:04:10,167 --> 00:04:16,641
Because the deeper we go, the more there is to say about these two pipes.

73
00:04:16,711 --> 00:04:20,274
As the water keeps falling, off the east side of Mackinac Island,

74
00:04:20,294 --> 00:04:23,691
something appears that makes no sense in the middle of a lake.

75
00:04:25,551 --> 00:04:26,131
A cliff.

76
00:04:26,201 --> 00:04:30,171
A wall of rock about 100 feet high.

77
00:04:30,241 --> 00:04:31,581
It's a waterfall.

78
00:04:31,651 --> 00:04:37,461
It was discovered in August 2007, and named Mackinac Falls.

79
00:04:37,531 --> 00:04:41,041
Except no water has poured over it for thousands of years.

80
00:04:41,111 --> 00:04:43,911
It sits at the bottom of a lake.

81
00:04:43,981 --> 00:04:47,691
So how does a waterfall end up 100 feet underwater?

82
00:04:47,761 --> 00:04:49,381
Hold on to that question.

83
00:04:49,451 --> 00:04:53,167
The answer is at the very bottom.

84
00:04:53,237 --> 00:04:58,052
As the water keeps falling, the Straits start to look like a graveyard.

85
00:04:58,122 --> 00:05:03,157
Thick fog and crowded shipping lanes have sunk ship after ship here.

86
00:05:03,227 --> 00:05:06,947
Half a dozen lighthouses stand around it for a reason.

87
00:05:07,017 --> 00:05:10,666
Today, this water is a protected shipwreck preserve, 148 square miles of it,

88
00:05:10,686 --> 00:05:13,997
with at least 22 wrecks that divers can visit, and more lying deeper.

89
00:05:18,977 --> 00:05:21,387
The Sandusky, at around 80 feet.

90
00:05:21,457 --> 00:05:22,247
The M.

91
00:05:22,317 --> 00:05:23,907
Stalker, at 85.

92
00:05:23,977 --> 00:05:27,267
The William Young, at 120.

93
00:05:27,337 --> 00:05:29,897
The Minneapolis, at 124.

94
00:05:29,967 --> 00:05:35,497
The Eber Ward, between 111 and 145.

95
00:05:35,567 --> 00:05:40,997
Now the whole of the Cedarville is out of the water too, lying in her two halves.

96
00:05:41,067 --> 00:05:44,137
Before we go any deeper, take a guess.

97
00:05:44,207 --> 00:05:47,597
What's the strangest thing at the very bottom of the Straits?

98
00:05:47,667 --> 00:05:48,587
A ship?

99
00:05:48,657 --> 00:05:49,907
The pipelines?

100
00:05:49,977 --> 00:05:51,327
Or something far older?

101
00:05:51,397 --> 00:05:54,267
Put your guess in the comments right now.

102
00:05:54,337 --> 00:05:55,877
One word is enough.

103
00:05:55,947 --> 00:05:57,667
Then see if you were right.

104
00:05:57,737 --> 00:05:59,177
And stay with me.

105
00:05:59,247 --> 00:06:03,558
The deepest part of the Straits still holds the answer to that waterfall,

106
00:06:03,578 --> 00:06:06,227
and the rest of the story of those two pipes.

107
00:06:07,503 --> 00:06:13,263
As the water falls toward 200 feet, the bridge itself starts to look different.

108
00:06:13,333 --> 00:06:17,123
It opened on November 1, 1957.

109
00:06:17,193 --> 00:06:23,903
It's 5 miles long, and its two towers rise 552 feet above the water.

110
00:06:23,973 --> 00:06:26,203
But they don't stop at the waterline.

111
00:06:26,273 --> 00:06:30,453
Their foundations go down another 210 feet.

112
00:06:30,523 --> 00:06:36,063
Each tower stands more than 760 feet from its base to its top.

113
00:06:36,133 --> 00:06:38,443
Drivers only ever see part of it.

114
00:06:38,513 --> 00:06:43,153
Building them took four summers, with no work in the winter,

115
00:06:43,173 --> 00:06:45,173
at a cost of $100 million.

116
00:06:45,243 --> 00:06:47,643
And five men.

117
00:06:47,713 --> 00:06:54,693
In September 1954, a diver named Frank Pepper came up too fast from 140 feet down.

118
00:06:56,093 --> 00:06:58,323
He died of the bends.

119
00:06:58,393 --> 00:06:59,462
A month later,

120
00:06:59,482 --> 00:07:05,373
James LeSarge lost his balance and fell 40 feet into one of the foundations.

121
00:07:05,513 --> 00:07:10,623
Two weeks after that, Albert Abbott fell from a narrow beam into the water.

122
00:07:10,693 --> 00:07:16,643
On June 6, 1956, a catwalk near the north tower collapsed.

123
00:07:16,713 --> 00:07:21,063
Jack Baker and Robert Koppen, both 28, fell.

124
00:07:21,133 --> 00:07:23,083
It was their first day on the job.

125
00:07:23,153 --> 00:07:26,543
Koppen's body was never recovered.

126
00:07:26,613 --> 00:07:30,393
People like to say there are workers buried inside the bridge.

127
00:07:30,463 --> 00:07:31,063
There aren't.

128
00:07:31,133 --> 00:07:34,563
But one of them was never found.

129
00:07:34,633 --> 00:07:37,393
And the bridge has taken lives since.

130
00:07:37,463 --> 00:07:38,875
In September 1978,

131
00:07:38,895 --> 00:07:44,443
a small plane flying through heavy fog hit one of the bridge's cables.

132
00:07:45,543 --> 00:07:49,673
The wings tore off, and the plane fell into the Straits.

133
00:07:49,743 --> 00:07:52,834
All three men on board were killed.

134
00:07:52,904 --> 00:07:57,864
Under the middle of the bridge, the water was 250 feet deep.

135
00:07:57,934 --> 00:07:59,244
Now it's gone.

136
00:07:59,314 --> 00:08:03,876
And here, at about 270 feet, the two pipelines reach their lowest point,

137
00:08:03,896 --> 00:08:06,294
lying across the floor of the Straits.

138
00:08:08,294 --> 00:08:11,174
Remember I said there was more to say about them.

139
00:08:11,244 --> 00:08:13,494
They're more than 70 years old.

140
00:08:13,564 --> 00:08:16,183
In 2014, Enbridge, the company that runs them,

141
00:08:16,203 --> 00:08:20,544
was found not to be keeping the pipe supports as close together as required.

142
00:08:22,904 --> 00:08:27,073
And the company says that, although the pipes are in good condition,

143
00:08:27,093 --> 00:08:29,784
their outer coating has worn away in places.

144
00:08:29,854 --> 00:08:36,524
Then, in 2018, a tugboat's anchor hit the pipeline and dented it.

145
00:08:36,594 --> 00:08:38,594
The dent was less than an inch deep.

146
00:08:38,664 --> 00:08:40,539
The pipe held.

147
00:08:40,609 --> 00:08:42,754
But it showed what could happen.

148
00:08:42,824 --> 00:08:45,144
And here's why that matters so much.

149
00:08:45,214 --> 00:08:49,524
Lake Michigan and Lake Huron aren't really two lakes.

150
00:08:49,594 --> 00:08:52,588
The water through the Straits keeps them at the same level,

151
00:08:52,608 --> 00:08:56,574
so scientists count them as one, the largest freshwater lake on Earth by area.

152
00:08:59,624 --> 00:09:03,448
And that water surges through here, sometimes east, sometimes west,

153
00:09:03,468 --> 00:09:06,604
at times more than 2.5 million cubic feet every second.

154
00:09:08,844 --> 00:09:13,817
A study by the University of Michigan found that a leak here could reach about 700 miles of shoreline,

155
00:09:13,837 --> 00:09:15,824
and up to 60% of Lake Huron's open water.

156
00:09:20,154 --> 00:09:23,200
Enbridge says the pipes under the Straits have never leaked,

157
00:09:23,220 --> 00:09:27,134
and that they're watched around the clock and inspected by underwater robots.

158
00:09:28,914 --> 00:09:33,274
Michigan sued in 2019 to shut this section down.

159
00:09:33,344 --> 00:09:36,384
Canada has stepped in to defend the pipeline.

160
00:09:36,454 --> 00:09:42,714
And a tunnel to carry it through the rock beneath the Straits has been planned since 2018.

161
00:09:42,784 --> 00:09:47,070
For now, the two pipes are still here, lying on the bottom.

162
00:09:47,140 --> 00:09:48,880
Now, the very bottom.

163
00:09:48,950 --> 00:09:53,330
295 feet down, the deepest point of the Straits.

164
00:09:53,400 --> 00:09:57,470
And with the water gone, you can finally see what this place really is.

165
00:09:57,540 --> 00:09:59,880
It's a canyon.

166
00:09:59,950 --> 00:10:03,260
About 9,000 years ago, as the last ice age ended,

167
00:10:03,280 --> 00:10:06,930
the lakes here were hundreds of feet lower than today.

168
00:10:07,490 --> 00:10:09,447
Where Lake Michigan is now,

169
00:10:09,467 --> 00:10:13,840
there was a smaller lake that geologists call Lake Chippewa.

170
00:10:13,910 --> 00:10:18,053
And its water poured out through a narrow canyon, right here,

171
00:10:18,073 --> 00:10:20,100
where the bridge stands today.

172
00:10:20,170 --> 00:10:22,850
There was a river under the Mackinac Bridge.

173
00:10:22,920 --> 00:10:26,000
And on its way east, it went over a waterfall.

174
00:10:26,070 --> 00:10:28,530
Mackinac Falls.

175
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:31,662
Then the water rose, and rose, until the canyon,

176
00:10:31,682 --> 00:10:35,580
the river and the waterfall all disappeared beneath the lake.

177
00:10:37,570 --> 00:10:38,457
In Lake Huron,

178
00:10:38,477 --> 00:10:44,550
archaeologists have found at least 60 stone structures on a ridge that is now deep underwater.

179
00:10:45,860 --> 00:10:47,884
They appear to be hunting blinds,

180
00:10:47,904 --> 00:10:52,840
built about 9,000 years ago by people hunting caribou on what was then dry land.

181
00:10:54,460 --> 00:10:57,220
The world under these lakes wasn't empty.

182
00:10:57,290 --> 00:10:59,670
People lived there.

183
00:10:59,740 --> 00:11:03,243
If that just changed the way you see the Mackinac Bridge,

184
00:11:03,263 --> 00:11:05,160
do two things for me right now.

185
00:11:05,230 --> 00:11:09,730
Tap the like button, and if you haven't yet, subscribe.

186
00:11:09,800 --> 00:11:11,597
For a small channel like this one,

187
00:11:11,617 --> 00:11:16,780
that's the difference between this story reaching a few hundred people or a few hundred thousand.

188
00:11:18,408 --> 00:11:20,398
Now let the water back in.

189
00:11:20,468 --> 00:11:22,728
The river canyon fills first.

190
00:11:22,798 --> 00:11:24,488
Then the waterfall disappears.

191
00:11:24,558 --> 00:11:26,488
Then the pipelines.

192
00:11:26,558 --> 00:11:31,498
The water climbs the bridge towers until only the part we know is left above it.

193
00:11:31,568 --> 00:11:37,198
The Cedarville slips under, then the top of her hull, 35 feet down.

194
00:11:37,268 --> 00:11:41,888
Then the Rock Maze, and the lake is calm again.

195
00:11:41,958 --> 00:11:45,748
Tomorrow, thousands of cars will cross this bridge.

196
00:11:45,818 --> 00:11:48,108
Freighters will pass under it.

197
00:11:48,178 --> 00:11:52,588
And almost nobody will think about what's lying in the dark below them.

198
00:11:52,658 --> 00:11:56,560
If you live near the Straits, or you've crossed the bridge, dived these wrecks,

199
00:11:56,580 --> 00:11:59,638
or worked on the boats here, I'd really like to hear from you.

200
00:12:02,178 --> 00:12:04,378
What do you know about what's down there?

201
00:12:04,448 --> 00:12:06,018
Tell me in the comments.

202
00:12:06,088 --> 00:12:10,086
Some of the best parts of these stories come from people like you,

203
00:12:10,106 --> 00:12:13,068
and the best ones might end up in a future video.

204
00:12:14,862 --> 00:12:16,712
So here's my question for you.

205
00:12:16,782 --> 00:12:19,435
Two oil pipelines, more than 70 years old,

206
00:12:19,455 --> 00:12:23,762
lying on the bottom of the largest freshwater lake on Earth by area.

207
00:12:25,472 --> 00:12:27,012
Should they stay where they are?

208
00:12:27,082 --> 00:12:29,702
Move into a tunnel under the rock?

209
00:12:29,772 --> 00:12:31,742
Or be shut down?

210
00:12:31,812 --> 00:12:33,772
Tell me what you think in the comments.

211
00:12:33,842 --> 00:12:35,332
I read every one.

212
00:12:35,402 --> 00:12:37,956
And if you've watched all the way to here,

213
00:12:37,976 --> 00:12:40,652
you're exactly who this channel is made for.

214
00:12:40,722 --> 00:12:45,442
So if you haven't subscribed yet, do it now, and tap the bell.

215
00:12:45,512 --> 00:12:49,018
Because next, we're going to drain the Graveyard of the Great Lakes,

216
00:12:49,038 --> 00:12:52,492
the stretch of Lake Superior where the Edmund Fitzgerald went down.

217
00:12:54,412 --> 00:12:56,872
I'd like you to be there when we do.

218
00:12:56,942 --> 00:13:00,722
Thanks for watching.
